+\subsection{Blinded Human Review of Discrepancies and Edits}
+\label{sec:eval:human}
+
+We separately audited silver-reference discrepancies (task D) and actual
+system edits (task E). Task D is a seed-42 random sample of 200 occurrences
+from the 302 multiset discrepancies between natural extraction and the
+structured-field reference. Task E is a simple random sample of 200 edit
+occurrences from 598 edits across five configurations; it was not balanced by
+configuration. The unit in both tasks is a discrepancy or edit, so a document
+may contribute several items. Methods and reference-quality hints were hidden
+from annotators. Each item showed the source text, schema, input graph, and
+specified operation; task E also showed the full output graph.
+
+Two annotators with computer-science backgrounds and sufficient Chinese
+reading ability reviewed the instructions and practice examples, then labeled
+the same 400 items independently without discussing formal items. For each
+item, they separately judged whether the input had an error at the specified
+position (\texttt{is\_error}) and whether that particular add or remove
+operation was acceptable (\texttt{repair\_acceptable}), using 0, 1, or U
+for insufficient evidence and writing reasons. A third person adjudicated
+the 154 items with at least one disagreement by selecting between the two
+annotators' judgments after reviewing the displayed source and graphs. The
+246 fully agreed items retained their common labels; original labels and
+pre-adjudication agreement were preserved. One edit-acceptability judgment
+remained U after adjudication.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\centering
+\caption{Human review. Agreement and Cohen's $\kappa$ use the original
+0/1/U labels before adjudication. Positive counts use adjudicated labels;
+rates exclude U from the denominator.}
+\label{tab:human_review}
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}
+\toprule
+Task & Judgment & Agreement & $\kappa$ & Positive / certain & U \\
+\midrule
+D & Input error & 80.0\% & 0.600 & 95/200 (47.5\%) & 0 \\
+D & Suggested edit acceptable & 62.0\% & 0.334 & 86/200 (43.0\%) & 0 \\
+E & Input error & 86.0\% & 0.677 & 134/200 (67.0\%) & 0 \\
+E & Actual edit acceptable & 65.0\% & 0.128 & 134/199 (67.3\%) & 1 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+Only 95 of the 200 sampled silver-reference discrepancies were judged actual
+input errors, and 86 of their suggested single edits were acceptable. In the
+separate actual-edit sample, 134 of 199 determinate edits were acceptable.
+These are different conditional quantities: a valid missing fact need not make
+an isolated add acceptable if it leaves a second value in a single-valued
+field. Agreement was lower for edit acceptability than for input errors,
+especially in task E ($\kappa=0.128$); the adjudicated rates do not replace
+the original agreement measures.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\centering
+\caption{Task E acceptance by configuration in the unbalanced random edit
+sample. Rates use determinate edits only.}
+\label{tab:human_review_by_config}
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
+\toprule
+Configuration & Sampled & Acceptable & U \\
+\midrule
+Archived Simple & 105 & 63/104 (60.6\%) & 1 \\
+Archived Diagnosis + Gate & 41 & 25/41 (61.0\%) & 0 \\
+Gemma Base + Gate & 16 & 13/16 (81.3\%) & 0 \\
+Gemma Diagnosis + Gate & 16 & 13/16 (81.3\%) & 0 \\
+Gemma SHACL Context + Gate & 22 & 20/22 (90.9\%) & 0 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The configuration counts reflect how often each system edited in the sampled
+pool, not equal-sized method groups. The small Gemma cells do not establish
+rankings. Neither task provides whole-graph human F1, overall defect recall,
+or a prevalence estimate for all extracted graphs.
+
